--TEST--
A raw `ESC` byte written inside a string literal does not compile, in a double-quoted string, a single-quoted one, a heredoc and a nowdoc, and the error is at the byte itself — rule:tooling/an-escape-in-source-text-is-a-compile-error
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Each string below contains the `ESC` byte itself. It is invisible in most editors.
echo "[31mred", "\n";
echo '[1mbold', "\n";
echo <<<TEXT
    [4munder
    TEXT;
echo <<<'TEXT'
    [0mplain
    TEXT;
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0011]: this string contains the `ESC` character. To style terminal output, use `Core\Cli\Text`
  --> %s:3:7
  |
3 | echo "␛[31mred", "\n";
  |       ^ writes the `ESC` character

error[E0011]: this string contains the `ESC` character. To style terminal output, use `Core\Cli\Text`
  --> %s:4:7
  |
4 | echo '␛[1mbold', "\n";
  |       ^ writes the `ESC` character

error[E0011]: this string contains the `ESC` character. To style terminal output, use `Core\Cli\Text`
  --> %s:6:1
  |
6 |     ␛[4munder
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^... writes the `ESC` character
  | (continues to line 7)

error[E0011]: this string contains the `ESC` character. To style terminal output, use `Core\Cli\Text`
  --> %s:9:5
  |
9 |     ␛[0mplain
  |     ^ writes the `ESC` character

error: aborting due to 4 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
